The lazy answers of 16 KiB-signed images (cs-lazy=1) are kept in chunks of one page instead of four.

vm.cs_native4k_stats of the last desktop boot: "auth 43685 ... table 36224K peak 36224K" with 443 lazy blobs. The
table bytes are the per-blob pointer tables (at least one page each) and the chunks of 127 blocks, 16 KiB each, that
cshash-native4k-lazy.patch allocates wired at the first authentication of one of their blocks. About 34 MiB of chunks
held 43685 answers, about 20 per chunk: more than 80 % of the chunk memory held no answer, because the blocks a boot
uses lie scattered over the 6.8 GiB of the dyld shared cache. A chunk is now one page of 31 blocks (496 KiB of file).

What is read, hashed, compared, recorded and answered is unchanged, and so is the protection of the chunks (read-only
mapping, written through the physical aperture, one chunk per index installed by compare-and-swap). The pointer
tables grow by four (8 bytes per 496 KiB of file instead of per 1.98 MiB, about 110 KiB for all 6.8 GiB). There are
more and smaller kernel_map entries, one per chunk. How much is saved depends on how the used blocks cluster: between
nothing (no two used blocks of a 127-block chunk within the same 31) and three quarters of the chunk memory. The
table bytes of vm.cs_native4k_stats against "auth" x 128 bytes show it on the next boot. The layout is a compile-time
constant; no boot argument selects the old one.

Has not run on hardware.

